## Purpose

`pi-until` releases the current model turn while a cheap shell predicate waits or a recurring agent task approaches its next fixed-cadence tick. The owner is the current live Pi session. It is not a durable scheduler.

## Lifecycle boundary

- A normal turn can settle without stopping watches.
- `/reload` suspends active watch values to the session and restores them in the replacement extension instance before the original expiry.
- A graceful quit (`session_shutdown { reason: "quit" }`) writes a version 3 suspension entry with `reason` and the owning `sessionId`. The next long-lived process that opens the same session file (`session_start` `startup` or `resume`) restores those watches with no command, then appends `pi-until-resumed` to consume the entry. Expired, finished, duplicate, and already-active watches are skipped. Deadlines and recurring cadence stay anchored to the original start.
- `/new`, fork, a forked copy of a quit entry, a reload entry found at startup, a crash, SIGKILL, and reboot give no authority to restart. Print and JSON runs neither restore nor write a quit entry.
- `/until-resume` remains the manual path when automatic recovery did not apply.
- History: 2026-09-21 (`0.5.0`) made quit write an entry and added `/until-resume` as an explicit path. On 2026-09-22 Joel quit Pi for a runtime update. The drovr hawk session lost three watches because its process had loaded `cf28671` before `0.5.0` reached disk and never reloaded, so the reload-only handler ran at quit and wrote nothing. Even a written entry would have required a command nobody knew about. `0.6.0` makes quit-then-relaunch automatic. Version 2 quit entries left by `0.5.0` processes restore when they are newer than the session header and no message follows them.
- Same-process session replacement creates a fresh session delivery arbiter. A stopped arbiter is never reused.
- Session shutdown writes its suspension synchronously before awaiting restoration, claim settlement, or shell cleanup. Normal shutdown still waits for shell process-tree cleanup before publication.
- SIGHUP and SIGTERM synchronously journal and kill this extension's running shell process groups. A host signal handler gets time to settle its pending claims and ownership checks before publication. Without a host handler, one process-wide registry publishes all instances before re-raising the signal. A synchronous `exit` hook covers the dead-terminal exit 129; 143 is an explicit TERM exit used by RPC/print hosts, not a dead-terminal code. Default signal delivery emits no `exit` event. Unrelated crash exits do not create quit authority.
- After graceful settlement, a newly captured claim or confirmed foreign ownership can append a corrective suspension. The newest entry wins before the single publication. A payload keeps its transfer id across retries. Publication becomes committed as soon as its rename succeeds, even if the subsequent directory fsync fails.
- Emergency exit probes completed claim files and foreign ownership evidence synchronously. A libuv rename still in flight at process exit remains an unrecoverable window. An interrupted reload retains reload authority only, not automatic quit authority. A host that catches a signal and keeps running leaves pi-until stopped until reload.
- Exit persistence errors append only the time, session id and error code to a private log when storage permits. Commands, output, watch definitions and error messages are excluded. Startup maintenance removes the log after the handoff retention period or above 64 KiB.
- The shared signal registry excludes signal-exit v3/v4 emitter counts from host-handler detection. V3 stores its emitter on `process.__signal_exit_emitter__`; v4 stores it on `globalThis[Symbol.for("signal-exit emitter")]`. Those observers wait for other listeners before re-raising; counting them as hosts would swallow signals indefinitely. The registry observes ordinary host handlers before they can remove themselves during disposal. Its shared key and shape are a backward-compatible protocol, not a package-version key.
- A host still alive five seconds after a caught signal produces one local `signal-held` telemetry event per stopped session. `/until-stats` reports that the host held the signal for ≥5 s, not that it necessarily stayed alive indefinitely. Each delayed callback is guarded, and its session id is captured at installation rather than read from a potentially stale context. Teardown cancels the delayed observation; a failed non-quit shutdown still detaches its client.
- The shutdown boundary is claimed before journal I/O. A failed write still cancels actors and kills running checks. Hooks retry from frozen pre-cancellation values only while the context remains live. After a graceful quit with a failed initial or corrective journal, Pi invalidates the context before exit. There is no exit-time recovery guarantee. Inspect shutdown/publication notices and the private error log, then use `/until-resume` for available saved work; no command can restore an unwritten suspension. The stale-context settlement test asserts the logged failure, not publication.
- A waiting successor lists a separate pending inventory: ids, labels, watch expiry, predecessor, and handoff deadline. The fork snapshot is advisory, not permission to start actors. Unknown inventory stays visibly pending rather than inviting re-arming.

Pi `0.84.2` supports this boundary through `session_shutdown`, `session_start`, `agent_settled`, `message_start`, `ctx.isIdle()`, custom session entries, and follow-up messages. The compaction hold also needs `session_before_compact`, `session_compact`, and `session_compact_failed`. Pi added `session_compact_failed` in `0.85`, so the dev pins moved to `0.87.1`, the fleet runtime. On older Pi, a failed compaction holds wakes until the next `agent_settled`.

## Two machines

Each watch is an XState v5 actor. It owns cadence, gate checks, pending delivery, settlement, completion, expiry, cancellation, and typed terminal failure.

One session XState actor owns all `pi-until` delivery. It:

- queues due watches without submitting two follow-ups to Pi at once;
- deduplicates recurring ticks by watch;
- stamps dispatch time before packet rendering;
- correlates `message_start` through `details.followUpId`;
- waits for the resulting `agent_settled` before dispatching the next item;
- pauses in `startUncertain` when acknowledgement takes more than five seconds;
- reconciles a late acknowledgement without changing the packet's cadence facts;
- fails only on explicit dispatch rejection;
- drops queued work whose watch is no longer live;
- holds dispatch while Pi compacts outside an agent run, then waits a five-second grace window so a prompt queued behind compaction starts first.

### Compaction hold

On 2026-09-25 Joel reported that short-interval watches disturbed compaction in the drovr hawk session. Pi `0.87.1` `sendCustomMessage(..., { triggerTurn: true })` starts `_runAgentPrompt` whenever no agent run is active. `prompt()` refuses input during manual compaction, but that path has no guard. Manual compaction first aborts the run and then summarizes outside one, so the arbiter sat in `ready` and dispatched wakes into it. The hawk session has one compaction: extension-provided, at 751,810 tokens on a 1M-window model, far below the threshold, so manual. Wakes ran full tool turns during the summary. When it landed, the bridge tore down the in-flight query, the wake's turn ended as an empty `aborted` assistant, and the next wake started 120 ms later. A real Pi RPC run with a slow faux summarizer reproduced the turn inside `compaction_start`/`compaction_end`. With the hold, the wake arrived five seconds after `compaction_end`.

Compaction inside an agent run needs no hold: threshold, overflow, and between-tool-step compaction happen while the arbiter is already busy. Checks keep running during a hold; only dispatch waits. `ctx.isIdle()` is false while Pi compacts, so starting a watch then must not mark the session busy, or the queue would wait for a turn that never comes. Pre-prompt compaction after an aborted run has the same shape as manual compaction and gets the same hold.

An acknowledgement timeout is uncertainty, not proof that Pi rejected the message. Sending another follow-up at that point could stack agent turns.

## Agent contract

- `start` waits for a side-effect-free shell predicate. Exit code 0 is the only true result.
- Conditions must fail closed. On 2026-09-28 two sessions got false-success wakes on check 1 from conditions that exited 0 on missing input, including an empty script written in the same tool batch that armed the watch, and conditions that read stale output or a dead pid file because the call that reset them ran in the same parallel batch. A marker-line success mode was rejected because it would split the single exit-code gate; `grep -qx MARKER file` already expresses it. The fix was guidance in the README and tool text. An optional first-check delay is pending Joel's decision.
- `repeat` asks the same agent to do work on a fixed cadence. A gate permits a tick but never completes the task.
- `instruction`, `quickRef`, `contextRefs`, and origin are immutable private session data.
- Context references remain opaque until the waking agent needs one.
- Only explicit `complete`, `cancel`, typed failure, or expiry ends a recurrence.
- An expired or failed packet is inactive. The agent does not continue it without a new user request.
- Reading a finished watch with `status` or `cancel` drops only that watch's queued terminal wake; `list` does not consume wakes, and already dispatched wakes still settle normally.

The public contract lives across `README.md`, `src/command.ts`, the registered tool guidance in `extensions/pi-until.ts`, and `src/packet.ts`. `AGENTS.md` names this set so future behavior changes update it together.

## Privacy and bounds

- Shell stdout and stderr never enter receipts or model context.
- Telemetry is local JSONL. It stores a 12-character condition hash, never command fragments, recurring instructions, quick references, or context pointers.
- The extension retains the newest 50 terminal receipts in memory.
- The flat tool schema has one `Type.Object` root for the OpenAI Codex bridge. `prepareArguments` repairs only known string encodings before normal validation.

## Rejected designs

- Global live actors across `/reload`: stale Pi contexts and split cleanup ownership.
- A daemon or reboot scheduler: violates session ownership.
- Per-watch delivery decisions: cannot enforce session-wide serialization.
- Treating acknowledgement timeout as rejection: the accepted message can still start after slow `before_agent_start` hooks.
- Pausing checks during compaction: a check never touches the session, and paused checks would delay the wake without protecting anything.

## Terminal restart evidence (2026-11-11)

A real interactive Pi 1.5.0-p10 under a private PTY reproduced the failure with one inert watch. `ctx.shutdown()` and SIGTERM with a live terminal each wrote one suspension and one mailbox. Closing the PTY master exited 129 without either write. A fixture observer registered after pi-until's shutdown handler was never reached; this does not prove that teardown never began.

The installed interactive runtime handles SIGHUP/SIGTERM, but dead-terminal stream errors call `emergencyTerminalExit()`, which invokes `process.exit(129)` without extension cleanup. The extension runner awaits handlers in order, so an earlier handler or pi-until's own cleanup awaits can yield before the suspension is written. Moving only the session-shutdown write is insufficient; synchronous signal and exit hooks cover that gap.

`tests/live-restart.py` exercises interactive quit, quit during a shell check, PTY-master close, SIGHUP, SIGTERM, and direct emergency exit. Two explicitly targeted successors compete for each handoff. The proof checks one parent suspension, one successor adoption, preserved watch identity/start/expiry, and zero model turns. These common-path cases do not prove settlement races.

Review of the first packet found two settlement regressions: an in-flight claim could be stranded, and a transferred same-session quit could be re-suspended. `tests/restart-settlement.test.ts` holds claim renames before and after their filesystem effect, then checks third-successor adoption. It also covers a transferred reopen interrupted during ownership checking, and publication failure before or after rename. `tests/exit-hooks.test.ts` runs isolated Node processes to prove one signal hook and actual HUP/TERM termination for one or two module copies. It covers bare Node, signal-exit v3/v4 shapes, both shapes together, and the installed signal-exit module when `PI_UNTIL_SIGNAL_EXIT_MODULE` names it. Set `PI_UNTIL_SIGNAL_EXIT_COMPANION_MODULE` as well to cover real v3 and v4 together. `npm run check` now discovers npm's bundled v4 and the repository's v3 automatically; missing modules emit a loud coverage warning. A draining-host test keeps a stale client beside a live one through the five-second observation and successful publication. A watchdog exposes a swallowed real signal instead of allowing natural exit to hide it. A once-only host test proves settlement still precedes publication. Lifecycle tests cover caught signals and emergency exit during reload.

### Handback when an adopted successor is discarded

Do not equate `dropWatches` with handback. It does not say whether work was cancelled or relocated, and a running predecessor may still hold stale state. A safe handback needs an explicit ownership transfer from the current holder to one named recipient, a durable receipt consuming the current holder's work, and the same atomic claim protocol. Muster must name the recipient and distinguish discard from transfer before closing the pane. This is not a small extension-only change, so it remains deferred. Ordinary successor shutdown already forwards its owned watches and any pending predecessor chain.

## Current receipt

Version `0.6.0` adds automatic quit-then-relaunch recovery; see the PR receipt in the pi-until repo history. Version `0.4.0` passed 59 tests, `npm run check`, `npm run pack:dry`, a real Pi RPC recurrence, independent review, and GitHub Actions. The installed Git source is `git:github.com/joelhooks/pi-until@main`.
